 inflation at 3.7% year-over-year and core at 3.3%, combined with hawkish FOMC communications, form the main driver of current trader sentiment around Federal Reserve rate hike probabilities. The policy rate has remained in the 3.50%-3.75% target range since late 2025, with the July meeting featuring three dissents favoring a 25 basis point increase amid solid economic growth, resilient labor markets, and supply-driven price pressures linked to Middle East tensions. Markets currently price roughly a 33-44% chance of a hike at the September 16 FOMC meeting and near-certainty of at least one move by year-end, reflecting the gap between persistent inflation above the 2% target and the Committee's patient stance ahead of midterm elections. Upcoming August data releases and the next FOMC statement will provide key tests for whether elevated readings sustain or ease these odds.","context_requires_regen":true,"context_updated_at":"2026-08-27T14:31:45.343Z"},"version":"v1"}]
